---
import InsertLayout from '../../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../../utils/jsonld';
import { KIT_OFFERS, KIT_ON_SALE } from '../../config/kit';
import '../../styles/kit.css';

const canonical = 'https://toledotechnologies.com/kit/compare/';
const title = 'Data migration acceptance: in-house, Toledo Kit or QuerySurge?';
const description =
  'Choose a migration acceptance approach: your own procedure, the US$240/US$600 Toledo file-comparison kit, or QuerySurge enterprise validation. Scope, evidence and limits.';
const trackedPurchase = (paymentLink: string) => {
  const url = new URL(paymentLink);
  url.searchParams.set('utm_source', 'owned_search');
  url.searchParams.set('utm_medium', 'comparison');
  url.searchParams.set('utm_campaign', 'prod-migration');
  url.searchParams.set('utm_content', 'kit-compare');
  return url.toString();
};
const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Migration Acceptance Kit', url: '/kit/' },
    { name: 'Choose an acceptance approach', url: '/kit/compare/' }
  ]),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    mainEntityOfPage: canonical,
    datePublished: '2026-10-01',
    dateModified: '2026-10-01',
    author: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'Toledo Technologies',
      url: 'https://toledotechnologies.com/'
    },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'Toledo Technologies',
      url: 'https://toledotechnologies.com/'
    }
  }
];
---

<InsertLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogType="article"
  publishedTime="2026-10-01"
  modifiedTime="2026-10-01"
  heading="Who will account for the rows?"
  subheading="Choosing between an in-house acceptance procedure, the Toledo Migration Acceptance Kit and QuerySurge."
>
  <p class="kit-kicker">
    Migration acceptance · comparison · reviewed 1 October 2026
  </p>
  <p>
    You have an exported-data migration approaching sign-off. Start with the
    inputs you can actually inspect, the evidence the approver needs, and who
    will maintain the checks after this run.
  </p>
  <p class="kit-note">
    <strong>The Toledo kit’s ceiling:</strong> one source file against one target
    file, delimited text only, with both read into memory. No database, Excel, JSON,
    XML or Parquet input. It supplies evidence for your review; it is not an audit
    or attestation.
  </p>

  <h2>When each approach fits</h2>
  <h3>Keep an in-house procedure when you already own the checks</h3>
  <p>
    If your team has tested queries or scripts, documented mappings and an
    accountable reviewer, build on them. You control the inputs, transformations
    and acceptance rules. You also own the tests, maintenance, exception record
    and proof that every row was accounted for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with the <a href="/kit/acceptance-checklist/"
      >free acceptance checklist</a
    >. Agree keys, field mappings, exclusions and unresolved scope before
    writing a sign-off. Buying a tool does not make those decisions for you.
  </p>

  <h3>Use the Toledo kit for a bounded pair of exports</h3>
  <p>
    The kit supplies a standard-library Python comparison tool, a written
    procedure and a sign-off template. It accounts for source and target rows
    separately and writes JSON, printable HTML and a findings CSV. You declare
    the mapping, normalization and blank-value policy, then investigate the
    findings.
  </p>
  <p>
    It fits when you can produce delimited-text exports that fit in memory and
    want inspectable local reports. It does not establish that the exports
    include all the original data, model one-to-many transformations or check
    every business rule. You still decide whether to accept the migration.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/kit/sample/">Inspect the complete fictional acceptance packet</a> before
    choosing. Its input files, actual generated report and findings show what the
    kit produces and what remains a human decision.
  </p>

  <h3>Evaluate QuerySurge for broader enterprise validation</h3>
  <p>
    QuerySurge describes automated source-to-target validation, repeatable
    migration test suites and API-driven execution. Its integrations cover
    broader data sources than this kit’s file pair. That makes it a candidate
    when the requirement is connected systems and repeated validation across
    migration waves. Confirm your sources and operating requirements in an
    evaluation. <a
      href="https://www.querysurge.com/solutions/data-migration-testing"
      >Source: QuerySurge’s data migration testing page</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its <a href="https://www.querysurge.com/compare-trial-options"
      >official trial comparison</a
    > offers hosted cloud, downloadable and guided proof-of-concept paths. Those are
    different evaluation arrangements; check the current conditions against your environment.
  </p>

  <h2>Two rows can still hide a failed migration</h2>
  <p>
    <strong
      >Synthetic illustration, not customer data or generated tool output.</strong
    > Each file below has two records and a total balance of 30.00. Row counts and
    totals agree; record identities do not.
  </p>
  <div
    class="kit-table-wrap"
    tabindex="0"
    role="region"
    aria-label="Fictional source and target rows; scroll horizontally if needed"
  >
    <table class="kit-table">
      <caption
        >Two fictional records per file; balances in the same units</caption
      >
      <thead
        ><tr
          ><th scope="col">Source key</th><th scope="col">Balance</th><th
            scope="col">Target key</th
          ><th scope="col">Balance</th></tr
        ></thead
      >
      <tbody
        ><tr><td>101</td><td>10.00</td><td>101</td><td>10.00</td></tr><tr
          ><td>102</td><td>20.00</td><td>103</td><td>20.00</td></tr
        ></tbody
      >
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    With the key compared as written and no declared rejection, source 102 is
    missing and target 103 is an orphan. Ask who owns the discrepancy, whether
    it is an agreed exception, and what evidence is needed before sign-off. Any
    approach you choose must answer those questions.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare the purchasing arrangements, too</h2>
  <p>
    These products cover different scopes. A licence price alone does not
    compare implementation effort, validation coverage or total cost.
  </p>
  <p>
    As checked on <time datetime="2026-10-01">1 October 2026</time>,
    QuerySurge’s <a
      href="https://www.querysurge.com/product-tour/licensing-pricing-options"
      >published pricing</a
    > lists a named Full User at US$12,642 perpetual, or US$6,006 per user for a 12-month
    subscription with a two-user subscription minimum. It also lists packages, cloud
    hourly options and enterprise quotes. Perpetual maintenance is listed at 18% annually,
    with the first year included and subsequent annual increases. Check regional conditions
    and current configuration costs with QuerySurge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Toledo kit has two perpetual licence choices, with full source and no
    licence expiry. <a href="/kit/license/">Read the complete licence</a> and <a
      href="/kit/#price">the product’s purchase and delivery details</a
    >. Consultancy rights cover client work; clients receive the outputs, while
    the kit stays with you.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      KIT_OFFERS.map((offer) => (
        <li>
          <strong>
            {offer.name} — US${offer.price}, once.
          </strong>{' '}
          {offer.who}
          {KIT_ON_SALE && (
            <>
              {' '}
              <a href={trackedPurchase(offer.paymentLink)} data-astro-reload>
                Buy the {offer.name.toLowerCase()}
              </a>
              .
            </>
          )}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  {
    !KIT_ON_SALE && (
      <p>
        Checkout is currently unavailable. You can still inspect the sample and
        licence.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <p>
    Delivery uses an encrypted archive and a release key shown on the Stripe
    payment confirmation page. Do not rely on the receipt email to contain the
    key. Python and OpenSSL are required. Read the <a href="/kit/delivered/"
      >installation, delivery and receipt-recovery instructions</a
    > before paying.
  </p>

  <h2>Take one decision to your reviewer</h2>
  <p>
    Identify the exact source and target, name the acceptance owner, and list
    what will remain untested. If the bounded export comparison fits, <a
      href="/kit/sample/">open the worked packet</a
    > and judge the evidence yourself. If it does not, use your own procedure or evaluate
    a platform against the requirements the kit cannot meet.
  </p>
  <p class="kit-buy-terms">
    Written by Toledo Technologies, the kit’s vendor. QuerySurge facts above
    come from its linked official pages, checked 1 October 2026; fit
    recommendations are our assessment. This is not a hands-on benchmark or an
    endorsement by QuerySurge.
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
